Development of efficient adsorbent materials is key to the success of the ANG technology. Many different porous materials have been extensively examined and evaluated as potential methane storage media. The early studies have mainly focused on traditional porous materials such as zeolites and activated carbons. Despite relatively high packing densities, the low micropore and extreme hydrophilicity of zeolites limit its practical application in methane storage. As for activated carbon, difficulty in tuning pore shapes and sizes has limited the utility. Typically, traditional zeolites exhibit methane uptake below $100~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ (standard temperature and pressure equivalent volume of methane per volume of the adsorbent material; STP: $T=273.15\mathrm{~K~}$ , $P=101.325~\mathrm{kPa}$ , while most porous carbon materials show methane uptake in the range of 50–160 cm3 (STP) cm3.1,2
Recently, the Advanced Research Projects Agency – Energy (ARPA-E) of the U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) reset the new methane storage target to guide the research on adsorbent based methane storage.3 The adsorbent level volumetric energy density needs to exceed $12.5~\mathrm{~MJ~L^{-1}}$ and 9.2 MJ ${\bf L}^{-1}$ after packing losses at room temperature. This corresponds to a volumetric storage capacity of $350~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for the adsorbent material. Even if there is no packing loss, the volumetric storage capacity still needs to be higher than $263\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , equivalent to that of CNG at 250 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . The volumetric storage target is significantly higher than the previous one of $180~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at 35 bar.4 Additionally, DOE sets a target concerning the gravimetric energy density, $0.5~\mathrm{g}\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\right)\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (adsorbent). Accordingly, new porous adsorbents are required to meet these challenging storage targets in order for the ANG technology to become practical.
